## Units

| # | Year | Unit and compelling question | Days | Skills | Owned standards | Cues and settings | What students learn | Why this unit, here | Check set adds | Sensitive topics and teacher notes |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | 6 | Break down the throw. *What are the parts of a good throw, and how can a partner and I find the part to fix?* | 13 | g6-analyze-skill; g6-throw-roll-form; g6-rules-safe | SHAPE.S2.8.18; SHAPE.S1.8.6; SHAPE.S3.8.6; SHAPE.S3.8.7 | Cues: "prepare, act, follow through"; "eyes on target, step opposite foot, rotate the hips, follow through". Settings: partner watch with the cue card, drills to a wall and a target, then modified target games (a target ball game, a bowling-style roll game) | Break a skill into preparation, action and follow through, watch a partner and name the part to fix and its cue (S2.8.18; skill-analysis introduced on the first skill days); roll, and throw underhand, sidearm and overhand, with good form in drills and in two modified target games (S1.8.6, in full: all four actions in drills and in games); follow game rules in the drill, the target game and the roll game (S3.8.6) and name and practice safe behavior during activity and with equipment: one throwing direction, equipment checked, water in warm weather (S3.8.7). Opens with retrieval tasks on grade 5's striking and fielding throws and fair decisions, and readiness checks on g4-object-ideas (key parts of object skills) and g3-stay-safe-own (staying safe on one's own) in the last ten minutes of day 1. Ends: 2 check days (skill task: two throwing forms, three of five on target, the baseline cue "step opposite foot"; knowledge task: name a part to fix and its cue for a pictured throw; conduct: follows the target game's rules and equipment steps in two settings), 1 respond day. Tools: soft throwing balls, cones, paper targets, cue and analysis cards (Curio Class writes them); an optional skill clip on the teacher's device | First because skill analysis is the band's new model and every later motor row uses it, and the class rules and safe behavior must be set before any game; throwing is the grade 6 skill closest to grade 5's work, so analysis is taught on a known pattern | Candidate: a partner analysis card the pair fills for one throw | No human targets; one throwing direction; balls fetched on the signal. Hazards and controls: thrown objects (soft balls, lanes facing one way), collisions (marked lanes). Cost: $80 (30 soft balls, 12 cones, tape, paper targets; bottles for pins) |
| 2 | 6 | Fitness parts and the warm-up. *What kinds of fitness are there, and how should I warm up and cool down for each?* | 11 | g6-fitness-parts; g6-stretch-types; g6-skill-fitness-technique | SHAPE.S2.8.9; SHAPE.S2.8.14; SHAPE.S1.8.4 | Cues: "move to warm, then hold to cool"; "quick feet, soft landing, eyes up". Settings: a warm-up line-free circuit, agility and reaction stations with a partner, a cool-down routine | Name the health-related parts (endurance, strength, flexibility) and the skill-related parts (agility, balance, coordination, power, reaction time, speed) and tell them apart in a sort and one drill per part (S2.8.9); use dynamic stretches in warm-ups and static stretches in cool-downs and in training, building a warm-up and a cool-down (S2.8.14, in full: warm-ups, cool-downs and training); sound technique in skill-related fitness exercises: a tape agility ladder, a hop and balance sequence, a partner reaction drill and a power jump with no equipment (S1.8.4). Health's physical activity outcome 3 (warm-up and cool-down) is taught here. Opens with a retrieval task on grade 5's FITT and pulse-check (a pulse after the warm-up). Ends: 1 check day (skill task: one skill-related drill with its cue, the power jump's soft landing watched at baseline; knowledge task: sort ten activities and build a three-move warm-up and a cool-down; conduct: rules and safe spacing at stations, S3.8.7 review), 1 respond day. Tools: tape ladders, activity sort cards (ours) | Second because every later unit opens with the warm-up and closes with the cool-down this unit teaches, and units 3 and 10 build on the fitness parts | Candidate: each student's own warm-up card, kept for the year | Stretch to a gentle pull, no bouncing, no pain; static holds only after the warm-up. Power jumps on a dry, non-slip floor (Required access). Safety read (power jumps). Cost: $5 (tape) |
| 3 | 6 | Heart rate and strong muscles. *What does my heart rate tell me about my fitness, and how do I do strength exercises safely?* | 12 | g6-heart-rate; g6-health-fitness-technique | SHAPE.S2.8.15; SHAPE.S1.8.3 | Cues: "two fingers, count fifteen, times four"; "neutral spine, controlled speed, breathe". Settings: rest, walk, jog and circuit stations, private counts on the student's own sheet | Understand heart rate and what it shows about aerobic fitness: resting and working rates, beats per minute from a ten- or fifteen-second count, and why the rate for the same jog drops over weeks of training (S2.8.15); sound technique in health-building fitness activities: a body-weight squat, a push-up on knees or toes, a plank, a jog with posture and a held stretch after the warm-up (S1.8.3, in full: a variety, one from each health-related part). The count times four or six is multiplication from earlier grades; ratio words come from math grade 6 unit 2 (days 19 to 39), taught first (Cross-subject). Opens with a readiness check on g4-pulse-body in the last ten minutes of unit 2's last day, and a retrieval task on unit 2's fitness parts. Water during activity (health's physical activity outcome 4) is taught on the jog days. Ends: 2 check days (skill task: one exercise from each health-related part with its cue, the squat's "neutral spine" watched at baseline; knowledge task: count, convert to beats per minute and say what a lower working rate after training shows; conduct: takes a private count and keeps others' counts private, S3.8.7 review), 1 respond day. Out: target heart-rate zones as a training method (grade 9) | After unit 2 because the strength exercises are health-related parts named there and the warm-up is in place; after math's ratio unit, so beats per minute rests on taught ratio language | Candidate: a private two-week resting-rate log | Counts are private; no heart-rate score is a grade, posted or compared; a student with a heart or breathing condition follows the nurse's and family's guidance. Stop for sharp pain; no maximum lifts or counts; exercise is never a punishment. A mat or folded towel for planks. Sensitivity read sources: the federal physical activity guidelines' youth chapter and the national heart institute's page on physical activity and the heart. Safety read (resistance exercise). Cost: $0 (a clock with a second hand; mats from stock or towels) |
| 4 | 6 | Catch, pass and play together. *How do we keep the ball moving to each other, and how does every teammate help?* | 12 | g6-catch-form; g6-pass-receive; g6-welcome-differences | SHAPE.S1.8.8; SHAPE.S1.8.14; SHAPE.S3.8.1; SHAPE.S3.8.2 | Cues: "ready hands, eyes on it, give"; "lead the receiver, move to open space". Settings: partner catches at three heights with and without a scoop or glove, three-on-three passing drills, a three-on-three keep-away with everyone touching the ball | Catch with good form, with and without gear (a glove or a scoop), in drills and small-sided games (S1.8.8, in full); pass and receive while moving in small-sided games (S1.8.14); understand and welcome differences among classmates (ability, size, background, language) (S3.8.1) and show care and contribute helpfully to a team (S3.8.2). tactics-question first used here (where is the space, and what is my best move?). Opens with retrieval tasks on grade 5's invasion and fielding skills and fair decisions, and on grade 5's conflict solving for the team-talk days. Ends: 2 check days (skill task: catches a tossed ball at three heights, the baseline cue "give"; five passes on the move in a three-on-three drill, as the last Fluency day's closure watch, carried; knowledge task: name two ways a teammate can help a classmate who is new to the skill; conduct: a helpful contribution and an inclusive pass in the keep-away), 1 respond day. Tools: soft and playground balls, milk-jug scoops (low-equipment) or gloves, pinnies | Catching and passing need unit 1's skill analysis and throwing form; welcoming differences comes with the first team game of the year, where it first matters | Candidate: an "every player touches it" rule the class writes | Teams made privately by the teacher; no public picks. Ball sized for the group; no bare-handed hard-ball catching. Cost: $105 (15 playground balls, 30 pinnies; milk-jug scoops on the low-equipment path) |
