287: | 2 | 6 | Fitness parts and the warm-up. *What kinds of fitness are there, and how should I warm up and cool down for each?* | 11 | g6-fitness-parts; g6-stretch-types; g6-skill-fitness-technique | SHAPE.S2.8.9; SHAPE.S2.8.14; SHAPE.S1.8.4 | Cues: "move to warm, then hold to cool"; "quick feet, soft landing, eyes up". Settings: a warm-up line-free circuit, agility and reaction stations with a partner, a cool-down routine | Name the health-related parts (endurance, strength, flexibility) and the skill-related parts (agility, balance, coordination, power, reaction time, speed) and tell them apart in a sort and one drill per part (S2.8.9); use dynamic stretches in warm-ups and static stretches in cool-downs and in training, building a warm-up and a cool-down (S2.8.14, in full: warm-ups, cool-downs and training); sound technique in skill-related fitness exercises: a tape agility ladder, a hop and balance sequence, a partner reaction drill and a power jump with no equipment (S1.8.4). Health's physical activity outcome 3 (warm-up and cool-down) is taught here. Opens with a retrieval task on grade 5's FITT and pulse-check (a pulse after the warm-up). Ends: 1 check day (skill task: one skill-related drill with its cue, the power jump's soft landing watched at baseline; knowledge task: sort ten activities and build a three-move warm-up and a cool-down; conduct: rules and safe spacing at stations, S3.8.7 review), 1 respond day. Tools: tape ladders, activity sort cards (ours) | Second because every later unit opens with the warm-up and closes with the cool-down this unit teaches, and units 3 and 10 build on the fitness parts | Candidate: each student's own warm-up card, kept for the year | Stretch to a gentle pull, no bouncing, no pain; static holds only after the warm-up. Power jumps on a dry, non-slip floor (Required access). Safety read (power jumps). Cost: $5 (tape) |
288: | 3 | 6 | Heart rate and strong muscles. *What does my heart rate tell me about my fitness, and how do I do strength exercises safely?* | 12 | g6-heart-rate; g6-health-fitness-technique | SHAPE.S2.8.15; SHAPE.S1.8.3 | Cues: "two fingers, count fifteen, times four"; "neutral spine, controlled speed, breathe". Settings: rest, walk, jog and circuit stations, private counts on the student's own sheet | Understand heart rate and what it shows about aerobic fitness: resting and working rates, beats per minute from a ten- or fifteen-second count, and why the rate for the same jog drops over weeks of training (S2.8.15); sound technique in health-building fitness activities: a body-weight squat, a push-up on knees or toes, a plank, a jog with posture and a held stretch after the warm-up (S1.8.3, in full: a variety, one from each health-related part). The count times four or six is multiplication from earlier grades; ratio words come from math grade 6 unit 2 (days 19 to 39), taught first (Cross-subject). Opens with a readiness check on g4-pulse-body in the last ten minutes of unit 2's last day, and a retrieval task on unit 2's fitness parts. Water during activity (health's physical activity outcome 4) is taught on the jog days. Ends: 2 check days (skill task: one exercise from each health-related part with its cue, the squat's "neutral spine" watched at baseline; knowledge task: count, convert to beats per minute and say what a lower working rate after training shows; conduct: takes a private count and keeps others' counts private, S3.8.7 review), 1 respond day. Out: target heart-rate zones as a training method (grade 9) | After unit 2 because the strength exercises are health-related parts named there and the warm-up is in place; after math's ratio unit, so beats per minute rests on taught ratio language | Candidate: a private two-week resting-rate log | Counts are private; no heart-rate score is a grade, posted or compared; a student with a heart or breathing condition follows the nurse's and family's guidance. Stop for sharp pain; no maximum lifts or counts; exercise is never a punishment. A mat or folded towel for planks. Sensitivity read sources: the federal physical activity guidelines' youth chapter and the national heart institute's page on physical activity and the heart. Safety read (resistance exercise). Cost: $0 (a clock with a second hand; mats from stock or towels) |
289: | 4 | 6 | Catch, pass and play together. *How do we keep the ball moving to each other, and how does every teammate help?* | 12 | g6-catch-form; g6-pass-receive; g6-welcome-differences | SHAPE.S1.8.8; SHAPE.S1.8.14; SHAPE.S3.8.1; SHAPE.S3.8.2 | Cues: "ready hands, eyes on it, give"; "lead the receiver, move to open space". Settings: partner catches at three heights with and without a scoop or glove, three-on-three passing drills, a three-on-three keep-away with everyone touching the ball | Catch with good form, with and without gear (a glove or a scoop), in drills and small-sided games (S1.8.8, in full); pass and receive while moving in small-sided games (S1.8.14); understand and welcome differences among classmates (ability, size, background, language) (S3.8.1) and show care and contribute helpfully to a team (S3.8.2). tactics-question first used here (where is the space, and what is my best move?). Opens with retrieval tasks on grade 5's invasion and fielding skills and fair decisions, and on grade 5's conflict solving for the team-talk days. Ends: 2 check days (skill task: catches a tossed ball at three heights, the baseline cue "give"; five passes on the move in a three-on-three drill, as the last Fluency day's closure watch, carried; knowledge task: name two ways a teammate can help a classmate who is new to the skill; conduct: a helpful contribution and an inclusive pass in the keep-away), 1 respond day. Tools: soft and playground balls, milk-jug scoops (low-equipment) or gloves, pinnies | Catching and passing need unit 1's skill analysis and throwing form; welcoming differences comes with the first team game of the year, where it first matters | Candidate: an "every player touches it" rule the class writes | Teams made privately by the teacher; no public picks. Ball sized for the group; no bare-handed hard-ball catching. Cost: $105 (15 playground balls, 30 pinnies; milk-jug scoops on the low-equipment path) |
290: | 5 | 6 | Dribble, defend and find the space. *How do I keep the ball with a defender near, and how do I use space, time and force when the game keeps changing?* | 14 | g6-dribble; g6-defensive-ready; g6-movement-ideas | SHAPE.S1.8.15; SHAPE.S1.8.19; SHAPE.S2.8.1 | Cues: "head up, ball close, change pace"; "knees bent, on the balls of the feet, goal-side". Settings: cone courses, a passive then an active defender, an obstacle course, a tag game with no one out, a three-on-three game | Use a dribbling skill (hands and feet) in drills and small-sided games: control while traveling, change of direction, head up (S1.8.15); get into a ready defensive position and shuffle in drills and small games (S1.8.19); name how to use the movement ideas space, time, force and balance well when the setting keeps changing, in an obstacle course, a tag game and a small-sided game (S2.8.1, in full: all three settings). Opens with retrieval tasks on grade 5's defending ideas and problem solving, and on unit 4's passing. Ends: 2 check days (skill task: dribble through cones and past a passive defender, the baseline cue "head up"; the defensive stance and shuffle as the last Fluency day's closure watch, carried; knowledge task: name the movement idea used to solve a pictured problem in each of two settings; conduct: plays the no-one-out tag rule and gives a teammate space, S3.8.6 review), 1 respond day | After the mid-year check, so dribbling and defense rest on unit 4's passing; movement ideas sit here because the changing settings are the dribble and defend games | Candidate: an obstacle course a group designs for another | Tag is a light touch on the shoulder or back; no one is a target or out. Two-way traffic only after the space check. Cost: $0 (reuses balls, cones, pinnies) |
291: | 6 | 6 | Dances in many styles. *How do dances from different places use counts and phrases, and how does expressing myself change how much I join in?* | 14 | g6-dance-sequences; g6-express-variety | SHAPE.S1.8.2; SHAPE.S4.8.1; SHAPE.S4.8.3 | Cues: "count, level, transition". Settings: a line dance, a pole dance with tape lines then poles, a partner set dance, a group's own rhythmic routine | Movement sequences in a range of dance styles: eight-count phrases, transitions and a sequence of four phrases (S1.8.2). Picks: Tinikling, a Philippine folk dance from Leyte where dancers step between two bamboo poles (tape lines first, then poles on the floor, then slowly moving poles; no "national dance" claim, as the claim is not settled at source); the Virginia Reel, a US country dance in longways sets, with teacher-set partners and no gender roles; a four-wall line dance in eight counts of our own, with no cultural claim; and a group's own rhythmic routine. Explain how self-expression affects how involved a person gets (S4.8.1), and take part in many kinds of activity that improve health, recording the kinds taken part in this year (units 2 and 3 fitness, units 4 and 5 team games, this unit's dances) and saying which fits best (S4.8.3, in full); goal-cycle and calm-steps first used here (a goal for the routine; a calm step before performing for a small group). Opens with a retrieval task on grade 5's gym and dance sequence and review of experiences. Ends: 2 check days (skill task: a four-phrase sequence to the beat, the baseline cue "transition"; knowledge task: one way self-expression changed how a person joined in, and the kind of activity that fits best and why; conduct: welcomes a partner's ideas in the group routine, S3.8.1 review), 1 respond day. Tools: music on the teacher's device or the teacher's count; PVC poles or ropes; dance cards and each dance's source line (Curio Class writes them) | Dance needs a year of movement ideas (unit 5) for levels and transitions; S4 sits here because a performance is where self-expression shows, and the record looks back on the year's kinds of activity | Candidate: groups teach each other one phrase | Cultural content: Filipino and US folk traditions; taught with respect, no costume or imitation of a people; sacred or ceremonial dances never used (Cory, 2026-11-20). Spot-checked 2026-11-25: Tinikling described as a Philippine folk dance from Leyte, performed at schools and on national holidays (an encyclopedia entry and a dance reference page; the national culture commission's own page was not found in one search, so the agent studies the Philippines' National Commission for Culture and the Arts pages first); the Virginia Reel was not checked, so the agent studies a national folklife archive's entry first. Moving poles: low and slow, clappers kneel, no pole lifted above the ankle; dry floor (Required access). Performing is for a small group, never alone before the class unless the student chooses. Safety read (moving poles). Swap allowed. Cost: $40 (8 PVC poles; ropes or tape on the low-equipment path) |
292: | 7 | 6 | Strike, serve and stay active. *Which striking and net activities could I keep doing, and where could I find ways to stay active?* | 14 | g6-strike-net-toward; g6-ways-active | SHAPE.S2.8.16 | Cues: "ready, turn, swing, recover"; "toss, step, contact". Settings: a handheld forehand to a wall, a swing at a tossed ball off a cone, an underhand serve with a short tool over a low rope net; a sampler of lifetime activities | Work toward the striking and net skills grade 7 owns: a handheld forehand, a swing at a tossed ball and an underhand serve with a short tool, in modified rallies over a low net with light equipment, each begun toward its owner: SHAPE.S1.8.5 (grade 7, unit 11), SHAPE.S1.8.7 (grade 7, unit 11), SHAPE.S1.8.10 and SHAPE.S1.8.11 (grade 7, unit 12), SHAPE.S1.8.12 (grade 7, unit 12). List ways to stay active (team sports, individual sports, dance, outdoor, community programs, active transport), with ones that fit different interests, budgets and abilities, and how to find each (S2.8.16). Opens with retrieval tasks on grade 5's net and wall and striking skills and goals, and a readiness check on g4-reasons-strengths (reasons to move) at the end of unit 6. Ends: 2 check days (skill task: contact a tossed ball with a swing, the baseline cue "eyes on contact"; knowledge task: a personal list of five ways to stay active with how to find each; conduct: one swinger per space and courtesy in the rally, S3.8.7 review), 1 respond day. Tools: short paddles, foam bats, light balls, a rope net; community activity cards (invented for practice, no program or company names) | Last in grade 6: the striking tries hand straight into grade 7's striking units, and ways to stay active closes the year looking outward; it ends before the year-end check, which samples it | Candidate: a family "active in our area" card sent with the family note | Swing space cleared on every side; one swinger per space; stand beside, never in front. Activity lists respect cost and access: free options first; no student is asked what their family can afford. Safety read (striking tools). Cost: $125 (15 paddles, 8 foam bats, light balls, a rope net) |
293: | 8 | 7 | Throw for power, coach a partner. *What makes a throw go far and land on target, and how do I give feedback that helps?* | 12 | g7-throw-power; g7-game-plans-feedback | SHAPE.S1.8.9; SHAPE.S3.8.3; SHAPE.S3.8.4 | Cues: "hips, then shoulders, then arm; step toward the target". Settings: throws to a target, long throws, throws to a base; peer-watching pairs; a small-sided throwing game with a team plan | Throw with accuracy, distance and power in drills and small-sided games: to a target, a long throw, a throw to a base (S1.8.9, in full); talk over a team's game plan before and between rounds (S3.8.3); give and take one kind, specific comment tied to a cue in a teacher-set peer-watching task (S3.8.4). Catching the partner's throw keeps g6-catch-form in use (review). Opens with retrieval tasks on unit 1's throwing form and skill analysis and unit 4's welcoming differences. Ends: 2 check days (skill task: one throw each way, the baseline cue "hips, then shoulders"; knowledge task: which cue gives the power, and a team plan for a pictured game; conduct: gives and takes a specific comment in the peer task), 1 respond day. Tools: soft balls, bases, feedback cards with sentence frames (ours) | First in grade 7: it reopens the year on unit 1's throw through retrieval and adds power; feedback is taught on a skill the pairs already know, before the team games of units 9 and 11 | Candidate: a feedback card each student keeps | Arm warmed first; one throwing direction; soft balls for long throws. Feedback is about a cue, never a person. Cost: $0 (reuses balls and cones) |
294: | 9 | 7 | Attack and defend the space. *In an invasion game, how does a team make space, and how does the other team close it?* | 12 | g7-invasion-attack; g7-invasion-defend; g7-net-invasion-toward | SHAPE.S2.8.2; SHAPE.S2.8.3 | Cues: "spread, move without the ball, pass and move"; "goal-side, close down, mark or zone". Settings: three-on-two and three-on-three games, diagrams on a whiteboard or card, first tries at the grade 8 skills | Explain attacking moves that create space: spread out, move without the ball, make a cut, pass and move (S2.8.2); explain defending moves that close space and block opponents: person-to-person and zone, closing down, staying goal-side (S2.8.3); first watched tries at the skills grade 8 owns, a volley against a wall, a stick dribble, a shot at a goal and a cut to get open, each begun toward its owner: SHAPE.S1.8.13 (grade 8, unit 17), SHAPE.S1.8.16, SHAPE.S1.8.17 and SHAPE.S1.8.18 (grade 8, unit 16). Opens with retrieval tasks on unit 4's passing and unit 5's dribble, stance and movement ideas. Ends: 2 check days (skill task: a cut to get open, then a pass, in a three-on-two drill, the baseline cue "move without the ball"; knowledge tasks: name two attacking moves and point to the space on a diagram; pick a defense for a diagram and say why; conduct: plays a fair game with a team plan, S3.8.3 review), 1 respond day | After unit 8's game plans; attack and defend use grade 6's passing, dribbling and stance, and the first tries prepare grade 8 | Candidate: a play the team draws and then runs | No body contact rules stated; small courts; full-court only where space allows. Pinnies, never shirts off. Cost: $0 (reuses balls, pinnies, cones; sticks borrowed from unit 16's list or pool noodles) |
295: | 10 | 7 | Train smart. *How do I make an activity a little harder each week, and how does training change how hard it feels?* | 12 | g7-fitt-principles; g7-skill-fitness-use; g7-effort-aerobic | SHAPE.S2.8.11; SHAPE.S2.8.12; SHAPE.S2.8.13 | Cues: "a little more, a little at a time"; "rate it one to ten, try the talk test". Settings: circuit stations, a steady jog or walk at three paces, sport drills for two skill-related parts | Use FITT, progression, overload and specificity across different activities: raise one part of FITT at a time and match the plan to the goal (S2.8.11, in full: all four across at least two activities); use what is known about skill-related fitness in different activities: which parts matter most in a sport and a drill for each (S2.8.12); describe how aerobic fitness relates to how hard activity feels, with the one-to-ten effort scale and the talk test, and why the same pace feels easier over weeks (S2.8.13). The rate of effort or pace against time leans on math grade 6's ratio work the year before (Cross-subject). Opens with retrieval tasks on unit 2's fitness parts and stretch types and unit 3's heart rate. Ends: 2 check days (skill task: keep a chosen pace at "talk test" effort for the time set, the baseline cue "steady breath"; knowledge tasks: change a plan for a different goal and say why; two skill-related parts for a sport with a drill each; conduct: private records kept private, S3.8.7 review), 1 respond day | After the grade 6 fitness units it builds on; before the grade 8 personal fitness test, which needs these principles | Candidate: a two-week plan change the student tries in warm-ups | No maximum tests; increases stay small; effort ratings private, no ranking. Sensitivity read sources: the federal physical activity guidelines' youth chapter. Cost: $0 |

