296: | 11 | 7 | Swing, place and play fair. *How do I swing a long tool with a full pattern and place my hit, and how do we settle disagreements in a game we officiate ourselves?* | 12 | g7-long-tool-swing; g7-strike-tossed; g7-settle-disagreements | SHAPE.S1.8.5; SHAPE.S1.8.7; SHAPE.S3.8.8; SHAPE.S3.8.9 | Cues: "grip, side stance, shift, rotate, contact, follow through". Settings: a tee, a soft toss, a self-drop; zone hitting drills; a small self-officiated hitting and fielding game with no outs that remove a player | Swing a long-handled tool to hit with a mature pattern, with a bat, a hockey-style stick or a golf-style club on a tee or a tossed ball (S1.8.5); strike a self-dropped or tossed ball with a tool into open space, choosing a place to hit (S1.8.7); settle disagreements with teammates and with opponents using calm steps and team talk (S3.8.8) and use and respect the norms of good conduct: honest calls, a handshake or fist bump (S3.8.9). Fielding the hit keeps g6-catch-form in use (review). Opens with retrieval tasks on unit 7's first swings and grade 6's rules, and a readiness check on g5-conflict-solve at the end of unit 10. Ends: 2 check days (skill task: contact a tossed ball with a mature pattern, three of five, the baseline cue "rotate"; a hit placed to a named zone as the last Fluency day's closure watch, carried; knowledge task: the steps to settle a pictured disagreement; conduct: settles a real call and keeps the norms in the self-officiated game), 1 respond day. Tools: foam bats, tees (cones), soft balls | After unit 7's first swings and unit 8's team plans; settling disagreements fits a self-officiated game, the first of the year | Candidate: the class writes its own three good-conduct norms | Swing space cleared; one swinger per space; stand beside, never in front; foam balls. No role play of a conflict between students; the disagreement cards use invented players. Safety read (striking tools). Cost: $20 (4 more foam bats) |
297: | 12 | 7 | Serve, stroke and keep your cool. *How do I serve and rally over a net, and what do I do when the game gets frustrating?* | 14 | g7-serves; g7-strokes; g7-manage-feelings | SHAPE.S1.8.10; SHAPE.S1.8.11; SHAPE.S1.8.12; SHAPE.S4.8.8 | Cues: "toss, step, contact, follow through"; "ready, turn, swing, recover"; SEL's "stop, breathe, name it, pick a tool, go on". Settings: serves to a wall and over a low net, alone and with a partner, a modified net game with a choice of a more or less competitive court | Serve with the hand, underhand and overhand (S1.8.10, in full), and with a short or long tool, underhand and overhand (S1.8.11, in full), in drills and modified net and wall games; forehand and backhand strokes with a short or long tool (S1.8.12, in full: both strokes, both tool lengths across the stations); use several strategies to manage feelings and behavior during activity: slow breathing, self-talk, a short reset, asking for a role change (S4.8.8; calm-steps in full, in SEL's grade 6 words). Opens with retrieval tasks on unit 7's first serves and strokes and grade 6's self-expression, and a readiness check on g5-review-experiences in the last ten minutes of the mid-year respond day. Ends: 2 check days (skill task: five serves into the court, to a named zone, the baseline cue "toss, step"; a three-stroke rally as the last Fluency day's closure watch, carried; knowledge task: name three strategies and when to use each; conduct: uses one strategy after a lost point, watched, S3.8.9 review), 1 respond day. Tools: paddles, light balls, beach or trainer balls for hand serves, rope nets | After the mid-year check: serves and strokes need unit 11's swing control; net games bring the most lost points, so managing feelings is taught where frustration is real | Candidate: a reset card the student keeps in a pocket | Emotions: a student may step out for a reset with a role; it is never a punishment; the teacher page names the school counselor; PE does not treat mental health conditions. Sensitivity read sources: the national mental health institute's pages on stress in teens. Safety read (striking tools). Cost: $40 (10 trainer balls) |
298: | 13 | 7 | Change the dance. *How can space, time, force and relationship change a dance, and how do friends and choices keep people joining in?* | 13 | g7-dance-ideas; g7-people-interests; g7-challenges | SHAPE.S2.8.8; SHAPE.S4.8.2; SHAPE.S4.8.4; SHAPE.S4.8.5 | Cues: "same steps, new idea: unison, canon, contrast". Settings: grade 6's line dance and a circle dance changed by groups, a rhythm form with sticks or claps | Use movement ideas (space, time, force, relationship) to change different dance and rhythm forms: a unison section, a canon, a contrast, in a line dance, a circle dance and a rhythm form (S2.8.8). Pick: kolo, a Serbian chain dance danced in a circle at community gatherings (on UNESCO's intangible heritage list since 2017), taught in a simple step pattern, then changed with canon and contrast. Explain how time with others affects involvement (S4.8.2) and link choice and interests to how engaged a person becomes (S4.8.4): groups choose their dance style and roles; closely examine challenges faced alone and as a group in movement (fear of failing, space, a fitness gap) and test one fix in rehearsal (S4.8.5). Opens with retrieval tasks on unit 6's sequences and self-expression and unit 5's movement ideas. Ends: 2 check days (skill task: perform a sequence changed with one idea, the baseline cue "canon"; knowledge tasks: say which idea changed it; connect a choice to staying involved; name a challenge with a fix and say how the fix went; conduct: includes every group member's idea, S3.8.2 review), 1 respond day. Tools: music on the teacher's device or claps; rhythm sticks (rolled paper on the low-equipment path) | Dance returns each year (units 6, 13, 18, 22) so it is never taught once and dropped; involvement and challenges sit with group choreography, where choice and others shape joining in | Candidate: a canon the class performs in two halves | Cultural content: Serbian community dance tradition; taught with respect, no costume; no sacred use. Spot-checked 2026-11-25: UNESCO's Representative List entry "Kolo, traditional folk dance" (https://ich.unesco.org/en/RL/kolo-traditional-folk-dance-01270) describes a social chain dance that involves the whole community; the agent studies a Serbian cultural institution's page first. Challenges: fear of failing named kindly; no student's challenge is shared without consent. Swap allowed. Cost: $0 |
