docs/courses/social-studies-grade-4/frame.md:122:| 2 | Our neighbors: Canada and Mexico. "How are Canada, Mexico and the United States tied together?" | 22 | ss4-canada-mexico-research; ss4-trade-north-america | MA.4.T1.3; D1.5.3-5; D3.1.3-5; D3.2.3-5; D4.5.3-5; D2.Eco.14.3-5; D2.Eco.15.3-5 | Owned: D1.5; D3.1, D3.2; D4.5; D2.Eco.14, 15. Practiced: D1.1 to D1.4, D3.4, D4.1 | Research Canada or Mexico (people and languages, government, cities, land, industries) from maps, atlases, articles and digital sources; choose sources that fit the question by two tests (does the set hold more than one view, and what do each source's origin, kind and date say), and how a source's setting (where, when and for whom it was made) affects how useful it is for the question; fact and opinion; check one fact in two sources; present, and critique a classmate's explanation for order, detail and accuracy. Why the three countries trade so much, what crosses the borders, and who gains and who loses in each | Follows the political map of unit 1 and keeps Massachusetts's order (4.T1.3). Trade sits here because this geography sets up the comparison (the row's own reason). The research routine taught here is used in every later unit | Candidate: New York grade 5, comparing the governments of Canada and Mexico with the United States (one short comparison table) | Indigenous peoples of each country (First Nations, Inuit and Métis; Indigenous peoples of Mexico) named in the present, from their own and their governments' pages. Trade's losers named without blaming a country |
docs/courses/social-studies-grade-4/frame.md:123:| 3 | The first peoples of North America. "How do we know about people who lived here thousands of years ago?" | 18 | ss4-first-peoples-evidence; ss4-native-nations-sites | MA.4.T2.1; MA.4.T2.2; MA.4.T2.3; MA.4.T2.4 | Practiced: D2.His.1 (timeline), D3.4, D4.1, D1 | Competing theories of how people first reached North America (a land bridge, a coastal sea route) weighed against dated evidence of people here about 15,000 years ago or earlier; what digs, artifacts and climate data can and cannot show; a given timeline marked in thousands of years, on which students place two or three dates (they do not build it: the to-scale timeline is unit 4's, deep time grade 6's); Native culture regions on a map, and photographs of stone tools, pottery, mounds and cliff dwellings read for what they show about the societies that made them; Mesa Verde, Cahokia and other preserved places, and what they add to the full story of American life, and the nations who count their builders as ancestors and govern today (the Pueblo nations; the Osage and other nations for Cahokia) | Time order: the deep past comes before the voyages of unit 4. It needs unit 1's maps and scale, and it is the first unit where students write a claim weighed against evidence on a question with more than one defensible answer | Candidate: California grade 5, major pre-Columbian settlements (cliff dwellers, pueblo peoples, mound builders, Woodlands peoples) and how farming shaped them | Native nations' own accounts of their origins are named as their own, beside the archaeology, not ranked against it. These are ancestral places of living nations, not ruins of vanished peoples. Sacred or restricted knowledge left out. Flagged for the sensitivity read |
docs/courses/social-studies-grade-4/frame.md:124:| 4 | Voyages and conquest. "What did the voyages of the 1400s and 1500s mean for the people already here?" | 20 | ss4-primary-source; ss4-exploration | MA.4.T3.1; D2.His.9.3-5; D2.His.11.3-5; MA.4.T3.2; MA.4.T3.3; D2.His.1.3-5 | Owned: D2.His.1, 9, 11. Practiced: D1, D3.4, D4.1 | How historians study voyages from archaeology, maps, drawings, journals and objects, and what each kind adds; before using a source, who made it, when, for whom and why; two sources on one landing compared, with the check that one did not copy the other. Norse voyages and the evidence of their meetings with Native peoples; European voyages of the 1400s and 1500s and why rulers paid for them; each told with the Native nations it met. The history set (item level, decided here): the Norse at L'Anse aux Meadows; Columbus and the Taíno; Cortés, his Native allies and the Mexica capital (1521); one northern voyage (Cartier); one Spanish entrada into what is now the United States (Coronado, 1540 to 1542). A class timeline of landings and conquests drawn to scale in centuries, with a paired timeline setting Coronado beside Cartier's third voyage (1541 to 1542) for one explicit comparison of things happening at the same time in different places, each with the Native peoples it met | Time order: after the first peoples, before the country's growth. Grade 3 met the later 1500s and 1600s explorers; this unit reaches back to the earlier voyages, so the to-scale timeline joins both. Sourcing is taught here because these are the year's first primary sources of the past | Candidates: California grade 5, navigation tools of the voyages (compass, astrolabe, ship design); New York grade 5, the exchange of plants, animals and diseases between the hemispheres | Conquest and enslavement named plainly with the human side (MA.4.T3): Columbus's men enslaved Taíno people, whose numbers collapsed from violence, forced labor and disease; the conquest of the Mexica capital. Taíno descendants and Nahua people today. No role play of voyages or conquest. Flagged for the sensitivity read |
docs/courses/social-studies-grade-4/frame.md:125:| 5 | How the United States grew. "Who gained and who lost as the United States grew?" | 24 | ss4-westward-growth; ss4-mexico-lands; ss4-choice-and-force | MA.4.T4.1; MA.4.T4.2; MA.4.T4.3; D2.His.14.3-5; MA.4.T4d.2; MA.4.T4e.2; MA.4.T4.4; MA.4.T4.5 | Owned: D2.His.14. Practiced: D2.His.1, D1, D3.4, D4.1 | How the country grew from 1791 to 1898 (the Louisiana Purchase, Oregon, the war with Mexico, Alaska, Hawaii, Puerto Rico), mapped and dated; canals, roads and railroads; why people moved west and wagon-train life; each gain told with the peoples whose lands changed hands: Native nations; the people of Mexico; Alaska bought from Russia in 1867 with no say for the Alaska Native peoples who lived there; the Hawaiian Kingdom overthrown in 1893 by a group of businessmen backed by US sailors, and Hawaii annexed in 1898 although many Native Hawaiians signed petitions against it; Puerto Rico taken in the war with Spain in 1898; Alaska Native peoples, Native Hawaiians and Puerto Ricans today. Texas, New Mexico, Arizona, California, Colorado, Utah and Nevada as part of Mexico; Texas independence in 1836, with keeping enslaved people as one cause; the war of 1846 to 1848 from Mexican, Tejano, Californio, Native and US sides. Many came by choice; people from Africa were brought by force and enslaved; by the mid 1800s the country was divided over slavery's spread west, which led to the Civil War | Time order, after the voyages. The Mexico rows (anchor sub-topics 4.T4d and 4.T4e) are taught here, in their time, not inside the region units, so the war with Mexico sits beside the growth it caused. Ends on the division that grade 5 picks up | Candidates: California grade 5, the overland trails (Oregon and California) and the people who used them; New York grade 4, the Erie Canal as the case of a canal, using ELA grade 4 unit 3's canal set (about days 81 to 119), no second canal text | The war with Mexico: causes disputed then and now; more than one account, the record settling what it can. Slavery named plainly; people named as enslaved people. Land taken from Native nations and Mexico named plainly. The overthrow of the Hawaiian Kingdom (1893) named plainly, with Queen Liliuokalani and Native Hawaiians' petitions; the unit writer checks each fact against the record. No role play or simulation. Flagged for the sensitivity read |
docs/courses/social-studies-grade-4/frame.md:126:| 6 | The regions: states, land and resources. "Why do people in each region make and do what they do?" | 26 | ss4-states-and-statehood; ss4-state-maps; ss4-regions-resources; ss4-national-capital | MA.4.T4a.1; MA.4.T4a.3; MA.4.T4b.1; MA.4.T4c.1; MA.4.T4d.1; MA.4.T4e.1; MA.4.T4a.2; MA.4.T4b.5; MA.4.T4c.4; MA.4.T4d.5; MA.4.T4e.5; D2.Geo.1.3-5; MA.4.T4a.4; D2.Geo.8.3-5; D2.Eco.4.3-5; MA.4.T4b.4 | Owned: D2.Geo.1, 8; D2.Eco.4. Practiced: D2.Geo.3, D1, D3.4, D4.1 | The states of the five regions and the Caribbean and Pacific territories on a political map; one class timeline of admission dates, region by region; why statehood with its own constitution and a voice in Congress beat being a colony, and what a territory lacks today; a Northeast region map (MA.4.T4a.2) and a map of one state in each of the other four regions, each with title, scale, compass rose, key and the features its own anchor standard names (the Southwest map with Native nations' homelands, movements and reservations and early settlement); how people adapted to each region and how rivers, coast, soil and resources shaped towns, industry and trade (the Northeast as the worked case); why regions specialize and trade, following one worker and one business in the regional case (why each specializes, what each trades for, how the exchange helps meet needs; personal finance touch, no added day: how the worker's pay is split between needs, wants and saving); Washington, D.C., the buildings of the three branches named simply, its civic resources, and that its residents have no voting member of Congress | The region sub-topics come after the growth of unit 5, so students know how each region joined. Grouped by theme, not by region, because each row spans all five regions: this unit holds the map, admission and resource standards; unit 7 holds peoples and hazards. The capital sits here beside statehood and Congress | Candidate: California grade 5, the fifty states and their capitals located and named | The Southwest map includes Native nations' homelands, movements and reservations today, from the nations' own pages, with the Navajo Long Walk of 1864 as the worked case of a forced movement, named plainly. Territories' and D.C.'s missing vote stated as a fact of law, not argued |
docs/courses/social-studies-grade-4/frame.md:127:| 7 | The regions: peoples and hazards. "How do the people of each region live with each other and with their land?" | 24 | ss4-regional-cultures; ss4-disasters-response | MA.4.T4a.5; MA.4.T4b.2; MA.4.T4c.2; MA.4.T4d.3; MA.4.T4e.3; D2.Geo.7.3-5; MA.4.T4b.3; MA.4.T4c.3; MA.4.T4d.4; MA.4.T4e.4; D2.Geo.9.3-5; D2.Geo.12.3-5; D2.Civ.13.3-5; D4.6.3-5 | Owned: D2.Geo.7, 9, 12; D2.Civ.13; D4.6. Practiced: D1, D3.4, D4.1, D2.His.11 | Each region's peoples and what they built and gave: Native nations past and present, African Americans, Spanish and Mexican settlers, Europeans, Chinese, Japanese, later immigrants and recent arrivals, and why people moved where they did. Hurricanes, floods, tornadoes, drought and wildfires in each region, and one technological or human-made catastrophe compared with a natural one (for example a dam failure such as the Johnstown Flood of 1889); before-and-after settlement facts and a movement map showing who was displaced; how a disaster far away affects people here; how governments and citizens responded, one response traced as a policy (the problem, who proposed it, how it was decided, how it changed); one international comparison of a response, its challenge and the chance it created. The year's informed action: a written proposal to the school or town on one hazard response, judged against the policy the class traced (met in grade 2 as a class readiness plan; this unit adds the policy and the written proposal) | Peoples and hazards close the year because both need every region placed (unit 6) and the year's research and sourcing (units 2 and 4). Informed action comes last because it rests on the hazard research | Candidate: California grade 4, the Gold Rush drawing people from around the world to the West, as a regional-cultures case (Ellis Island and Angel Island, taught in grade 2, are only retrieved) | Exclusion and wartime incarceration named plainly where a region's story includes them (the Chinese Exclusion Act of 1882, Japanese American incarceration), beside what each group built. Disaster losses handled with care for students who have lived through one: no student is asked to recount a family's disaster. The action is the students' choice, checked against official advice. Flagged for the sensitivity read |
docs/courses/science-grade-7/frame.md:147:| 1 | Chemical reactions and matter. How can we make something new that was not there before? Anchor: a bath bomb fizzing away in water while its mass goes down. | 23 | s7-reactions-atoms | MS-PS1-5; MS-PS1-2 | Practices: developing and using models; analysing and interpreting data; engaging in argument from evidence. Crosscutting: patterns; energy and matter | The safety sheet retaught (about 1 day); each pure substance has properties (density, melting point, boiling point, solubility, odor, flammability) that identify it, compared before and after substances interact to decide whether a reaction happened (MS-PS1-2, in full); substances consist of atoms: some consist of discrete molecules, others form extended structures, such as a salt crystal, modeled both ways (the start of MS-PS1-1, completed in unit 3); in a reaction atoms regroup into new molecules and their number stays the same, so mass is conserved in a closed bag (MS-PS1-5); Dalton's model revised by evidence; smell receptors respond to molecules (a part of MS-LS1-8, owned in grade 8); marble worn by acid rain as a new case. Also met (literacy, no added days): RST.6-8.3 (the bath-bomb and property tests); RST.6-8.7 (property tables and particle diagrams with text); WHST.6-8.1 (the argument that a new substance formed) | The base's first grade 7 unit: the particle model from grade 6 grows into atoms and molecules, which units 2, 3 and 4 need. | Candidate: none beyond the expectations; no atomic masses or balanced equations (the PE boundary). | Eye protection for all mixing; student mixing is limited to the named bath-bomb ingredients; no tasting; closed bags vented away from faces. The base's flame tests (lessons 5, 6 and 9), hydrogen peroxide reaction (lesson 6), heating of water (lesson 9) and splitting water with a low-voltage battery (lesson 10) are teacher-only demonstrations, each under its own protocol (chemicals and concentrations, quantities, apparatus, gas and pressure controls, protective equipment, where students stand): goggles for all, hair tied back, a fire-safe surface, an extinguisher nearby, hot plates rather than open flames for heating water, students observing from a set distance. Where a school cannot run a demonstration, supplied observation and property data keep the comparison the lesson needs. Lesson 9's second base day is condensed into this demonstration (Days). Stand-ins: kitchen ingredients, a kitchen scale to 0.1 g, zip bags; no-kit: before and after mass data. Simulation listed: atoms regrouping in a reaction. Sensitivity read. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
docs/courses/science-grade-7/frame.md:148:| 2 | Chemical reactions and energy. How can we use chemical reactions to design a solution to a problem? Anchor: a flameless heater that warms a packaged meal with no fire. | 18 | s7-reaction-energy; s7-design-optimize | MS-PS1-6; MS-ETS1-4 | Practices: planning and carrying out investigations; constructing explanations and designing solutions. Crosscutting: systems and system models; energy and matter | Some reactions release energy to their surroundings and some take it in, read from the system's temperature and explained with the particle model; design, test and modify a homemade heater, varying the kind and amount of substance (MS-PS1-6, criteria of amount, time and temperature); criteria and constraints refined (review of 6.5's start of MS-ETS1-1); a model (a small-scale test, a table of results, a simple simulation) used to change one variable at a time toward the best design (completes MS-ETS1-4, started in grade 6 unit 2); designs compared and a choice made between them (builds MS-ETS1-2 and MS-ETS1-3, owned in grade 8). Reviews grade 6's thermal energy codes (MS-PS3-3 to MS-PS3-5) as the base unit page does. Also met (literacy, no added days): RST.6-8.3 (the heater tests); WHST.6-8.2 (the written build instructions of base lesson 9); WHST.6-8.1 (the design argued against criteria) | Second in the base: it needs unit 1's reactions and grade 6's energy-transfer and design work. | Candidate: none beyond the expectations. | Eye protection and gloves; calcium chloride and ammonium chloride only, small amounts, never tasted; the ration heater is a teacher demonstration in a ventilated room (it gives off hydrogen and gets very hot). Proportional test (is the temperature change proportional to the amount): if math 7 has not taught it, the unit checks for a constant ratio in a table with one worked example. Minimum supervised route: teacher-portioned pure calcium chloride/ammonium chloride, plastic cup devices, thermometer, eye protection and gloves, with an initial test, modification and physical retest. No-kit: supplied temperature records; construction and physical testing remain not yet observed, so no complete MS-PS1-6 result. Never assume an ice-melt product has suitable purity. Simulation listed: reaction temperature by amount. Sensitivity read. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
docs/courses/science-grade-7/frame.md:149:| 3 | Metabolic reactions. How do things inside our bodies work together to make us feel the way we do? Anchor: a middle school student with stomach pain and weight loss (a disease of the small intestine lining). | 25 | s7-food-molecules; s7-body-systems-food; s7-cell-respiration-growth | MS-PS1-1; MS-LS1-3; MS-LS1-7 | Practices: developing and using models; analysing and interpreting data; engaging in argument from evidence. Crosscutting: systems and system models; structure and function | Food contains molecules of different sizes and arrangements: starch and proteins have linked building units, fats a different arrangement of components (a glycerol part joined to three fatty-acid parts), sugars and water are small; students model molecules of very different sizes and contrast these large molecules with simple molecules and with an extended crystal structure, a salt crystal (completes MS-PS1-1); digestion data read before and after (indicator tests as an extension of MS-PS1-2); the digestive, circulatory, respiratory, excretory and muscular systems, with the nervous system, working together to bring food molecules and oxygen to every cell and take wastes away, and what goes wrong when the intestine lining is damaged (completes MS-LS1-3, started in grade 6 unit 6); inside cells food molecules are rearranged for growth and repair, or react with oxygen to release energy (MS-LS1-7), with mitochondria named; food and growth (the start of MS-LS1-5, completed in grade 8 unit 5); other animals use the same reactions. Reviews cells (MS-LS1-1). Also met (literacy, no added days): RST.6-8.1 (evidence cited from readings on digestion); RST.6-8.7 (health and digestion data with text); WHST.6-8.1 (the argument about the case) | Third in the base: it needs unit 1's molecules and grade 6's cells. Course-level check 1 follows. | Candidate: none beyond the expectations; no named steps of respiration (the PE boundary). | A real health condition: the case is a renamed student; no student is asked about their own health, eating or weight (the sensitivity agent reads the unit after studying the health sources in the brief). Glucose strips with eye protection; no tasting; no iodine. No classroom burning: supplied qualitative combustion observations and mass and gas records replace base lesson 11. Stand-ins: pharmacy glucose strips; no-kit: digestion records and source observations. Simulation listed: molecules through the body. Sensitivity read. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
docs/courses/science-grade-7/frame.md:150:| 4 | Matter cycling and photosynthesis. Where does food come from, and where does it go next? Anchor: the ingredients on breakfast food labels, traced back to plants such as a maple tree. | 28 | s7-photosynthesis; s7-synthetic-materials; s7-ecosystem-matter-energy | MS-LS1-6; MS-PS1-3; MS-LS2-3 | Practices: developing and using models; constructing explanations; engaging in argument from evidence. Crosscutting: systems and system models; energy and matter | Plants take in carbon dioxide and water and, with light energy in their chloroplasts, rearrange the atoms into sugars and give off oxygen, so most of a tree's mass comes from the air (MS-LS1-6), from plant mass and gas data; chloroplasts and mitochondria (a part of MS-LS1-2, owned in grade 8); why plants do not die at night or without food (stored food, respiration); natural resources changed by chemical processes into synthetic materials (sweeteners, plastics, medicines, fuels), one researched for what it is made from, the reaction and its effects on people and places, with about 1 added day for the research from a supplied source set (MS-PS1-3); matter cycling among producers, consumers, decomposers, air, water and soil, energy flowing in and out, inside a drawn system boundary (MS-LS2-3). Reviews 7.1 and 7.3 codes as the base does. Also met (literacy, no added days): RST.6-8.2 (summaries of readings on photosynthesis); WHST.6-8.7 and WHST.6-8.8 (the synthetic-material research); WHST.6-8.2 | Fourth in the base: it needs unit 3's respiration and molecules, and hands the food web to unit 5. | Candidate: none beyond the expectations; no steps of photosynthesis (the PE boundary). | Tasting syrup or sap only under the school's food policy with allergy checks. An indicator that changes color with carbon dioxide used with eye protection. Stand-ins: radish or bean seeds, sealed clear jars, a lamp; no-kit: plant mass and gas data. Simulations listed: matter through an ecosystem; a sealed jar ecosystem over weeks. Sensitivity read. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
docs/courses/science-grade-7/frame.md:151:| 5 | Ecosystem dynamics. How does changing an ecosystem affect what lives there? Anchor: tropical rainforest cleared for oil palm farms, and the orangutans that live there. | 32 | s7-resources-populations; s7-interaction-patterns; s7-ecosystem-disruption; s7-biodiversity-solutions; s7-define-design-problem | MS-LS2-1; MS-LS2-2; MS-LS2-4; MS-LS2-5; MS-ETS1-1 | Practices: asking questions and defining problems; developing and using models; using mathematics and computational thinking. Crosscutting: cause and effect; stability and change | Resource availability affecting individual organisms (growth and survival) and population numbers, from data showing numbers rise when food, water and space are plentiful and fall when scarce (MS-LS2-1); competition, predation and mutualism recurring across ecosystems, used to predict a change in an unstudied one (MS-LS2-2); arguments from data that a change to the living or physical parts of an ecosystem changes its populations, and judging another group's evidence (MS-LS2-4); a change in the students' own community (base lesson 5); comparing farming methods and corridors that keep biodiversity and ecosystem services while meeting people's needs, weighing scientific, economic and social constraints (MS-LS2-5; the start of MS-ESS3-3); a land-use design problem defined precisely, with criteria and constraints including harm to people and places (completes MS-ETS1-1, started in grade 6 unit 5). Reviews photosynthesis (MS-LS1-6). Also met (literacy, no added days): RST.6-8.8 (facts, research findings and speculation told apart in stakeholder texts); RST.6-8.7 (population graphs with text); WHST.6-8.1 (the argument for a land-use solution) | Fifth in the base: it needs unit 4's food web and matter cycling. Day 96 checks three older rows: animal behavior last used grade 3, disruption last used grade 4, and resource protection last used grade 5. Two full reteach periods on days 100 and 101 respond, with resource protection applied in the environmental-change period; all are inside the 32 days. | Candidate: none beyond the expectations. | Farmers and communities in Indonesia shown with their own reasons, no blame on them or on families' food choices; no brand names (the sensitivity agent reads it after studying the communities' own voice, brief). Optional base lessons 19 and 20 are extensions outside the days. Paper population cards; no-kit: published population counts. Simulation listed: an ecosystem population model. Sensitivity read. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
docs/courses/science-grade-7/frame.md:152:| 6 | Earth's resources and human impact. How do changes in Earth's system impact our communities, and what can we do about it? Anchor: floods and droughts happening more often in communities that used to be steady. | 30 | s7-resource-distribution; s7-climate-change-evidence; s7-population-consumption; s7-human-impact-design | MS-ESS3-1; MS-ESS3-5; MS-ESS3-4; MS-ESS3-3 | Practices: asking questions and defining problems; using mathematics and computational thinking; obtaining, evaluating and communicating information. Crosscutting: stability and change; cause and effect | Warming air and water raise evaporation and change rain; temperature records, carbon dioxide and methane levels and human activity data, questioned to separate correlation from cause: human activities, chiefly burning fossil fuels, are the main cause of the rise in global temperature over the past century, and natural factors are weighed and found smaller (MS-ESS3-5); minerals, fossil fuels and groundwater found where past geologic processes put them, most non-renewable in a lifetime (MS-ESS3-1): base lesson 10 with its extension taught in full (fossil fuel formation and distribution), plus a Curio Class addition of about 1 day on mineral ores and groundwater from maps and geologic evidence, explained with grade 6's rock-cycle and plate models; students then explain an unfamiliar resource distribution from that evidence; population and use per person raising effects on Earth systems unless technologies and choices change (MS-ESS3-4); a method designed to monitor and reduce one human impact on water locally, judged against criteria and constraints (completes MS-ESS3-3, started in unit 5; uses MS-ETS1-2); the resilience plan (base lesson 17) runs in three base days, not four. Also met (literacy, no added days): RST.6-8.1 (evidence from climate and resource readings); WHST.6-8.7 and WHST.6-8.8 (the short research behind the impact plan); WHST.6-8.1 (a cause claim judged against data) | Last in the base's grade 7: it needs grade 6's water-cycle and climate models and unit 5's start of MS-ESS3-3. Course-level check 2 follows. | Candidate: none beyond the expectations. | Climate change taught as the evidence shows (BASELINE rule 5), with no "some say" softening; worry is met with solutions and data. Community water problems can touch families: focus on solutions, not blame. Percent change: if math 7 has not taught percent increase, the unit uses "times as much" and a supplied percent. Stand-ins: cups of water under a lamp; no-kit: public temperature and gas records. Simulation listed: carbon in and out of the air against temperature. Sensitivity read. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
docs/courses/math-grade-6/frame.md:122:| 1 | Area and surface area. How can we find the area of a shape we have no formula for? | 18 | m6-polygon-area; m6-surface-area-nets | 6.G.A.1; 6.G.A.4 | None (math). Practices: MP7 (structure: cut and rearrange), MP4 (model a real design with nets and areas, IM 6.1.19) | Area by decomposing, rearranging and enclosing; bases and heights of parallelograms and of all triangles (height may fall outside); A = bh and A = (1/2)bh explained, then trapezoids and other polygons; polyhedra, nets of prisms and pyramids, surface area as the sum of face areas in real problems; "squared" and "cubed" as exponents 2 and 3 for square and cubic units (starts 6.EE.A.1, owned in unit 6); evaluating the area formulas at given values (applies 6.EE.A.2c, owned in unit 6); the unit ends with IM 6.1.19, Designing a Tent, the culminating application of 6.G.A.1 and 6.G.A.4 to a real problem (MP4). Opens with retrieval on area with fraction sides (m5-fraction-area); retrieves square versus cubic units on day 11 and cube layers, volume formulas and powers of ten on day 14. | IM opens grade 6 here: it rests only on grade 5 area and shapes, gives every student a hands-on start, and its formula work retrieves grade 5 numerical expressions (m5-expressions) and begins this year's work with letters in formulas, which unit 6 names as variables. Supporting cluster 6.G.A, kept short and tied to the expression work. | Candidate: settled. Kept: Geometry progression comparison, zero student days. Kept: fabric-limit tent extension inside day 16, zero additional days. | Paper path: grid paper, scissors, nets to fold. No circles (grade 7). Sensitivity read. Trapezoid keeps the inclusive definition of grades 4 and 5 (at least one pair of parallel sides), so a parallelogram is a trapezoid; tasks say so where it changes an answer |
docs/courses/math-grade-6/frame.md:123:| 2 | Introducing ratios. What does it mean for two mixtures to be the same? | 21 | m6-ratio-language; m6-equivalent-ratios; m6-ratio-parts-problems | 6.RP.A.1; 6.RP.A.3a; 6.RP.A.3 | None (math). Practices: MP2 (reason with quantities), MP5 (choose a table, double number line or tape diagram) | Ratio language ("for every", "to", a : b) kept apart from fraction notation; discrete and tape diagrams; equivalent ratios by multiplying both quantities; the double number line and the ratio table, introduced here; "how much for one" and constant speed as the first unit rates (starts 6.RP.A.2, owned in unit 3); ratio pairs plotted in the first quadrant on a line through the origin; comparing two ratios; part-part-whole problems with a tape diagram; a Fermi problem. Ratio and rate reasoning begins here (parent and part a) and finishes in unit 3 (parts b, c, d). Expected error: adding the same amount to both quantities. | Ratio reasoning is the grade's first major cluster and the base of units 3, 6 and grade 7; it needs only grade 5 scaling and first-quadrant graphing. IM's chart puts 6.2 before 6.3 and 6.6. | Candidate: settled. Kept: ratio progression comparison, zero student days. Dropped: a second Fermi investigation, since day 37 already provides the owned reasoning. | Recipes and paint mixtures as contexts; food contexts avoid dieting or body size. Sensitivity read. Standards in full: 6.RP.A.3b, 6.RP.A.3c, 6.RP.A.3d. |
