d-of-unit evidence (two check days), id by id: DA:Cr3.1.7a, a guide look-for on the showing of the revised study, with the kept change log (revisions weighed, the standard adjusted, how each kept change clarifies intent); DA:Cr3.1.7b, the kept notation card of an eight-count sequence, scored on whether a partner can read it back; DA:Re7.1.7a, a written item on the unseen teacher-danced phrase beside one studied dance: their patterns and links compared; DA:Re7.1.7b, a written item: one element contrasted across the two styles in two specialized terms; DA:Re8.1.7a, a written item on two short written descriptions: what each dance means and one element or setting that shapes each; DA:Re9.1.7a, the revised written comparison of Esplanade and the kathak piece with the refined criteria card, scored after its revision day. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: an excerpt notated from a longer dance (grade 8). Tools: the notation card, the change log, the comparison frame, work cards, Curio Class's own; a notation practice tool or its printed card. Free path: printed symbol sheets. Once-a-week path: 6 sessions | After unit 6 because it revises unit 6's study; comparing meanings needs unit 5's quality words. The dance-notation model starts here, where it is first needed | Candidate: the Dance Notation Bureau's pages checked open and free on the day the unit plan is written | Kathak grew in part from temple storytellers and later the courts; the unit names it, uses a non-devotional piece, studies it by watching and its gestures by the source's own teaching, and no student imitates a deity or a dress; the sensitivity agent reads a cultural body's or the teacher's own published words first. Recordings for notation are optional, of feet and bodies with no faces unless the family says yes, and stay on the school's device. Hazards and controls: kathak spins not tried (turns kept to one slow turn in place); floor checked. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
