ence (two check days), id by id: DA:Cr3.1.8a, a guide look-for on the showing of the revised dance, with the kept revision note (feedback used and how each change strengthens intent); DA:Cr3.1.8b, the kept notation of a 16-count excerpt, scored on whether a partner can read it back; DA:Re7.1.8a, a written item on the unseen teacher-danced phrase: its patterns and how they relate to its stated purpose (shown at the teacher's spot instead if the student chooses); DA:Re7.1.8b, a written item: the unseen phrase and one studied style compared on how one element expresses intent, in two specialized terms; DA:Re8.1.8a, the revised analysis of the chosen dance, scored after its revision day on elements tied to expression with at least three named moments; DA:Re9.1.8a, the same analysis's closing judgment, scored on criteria weighing content, context and style. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: two notation methods compared (Level 1). Tools: the analysis frame, the notation card, the revision note, work cards, Curio Class's own. Free path: printed descriptions of each dance on the list. Once-a-week path: 6 sessions | After unit 10 because it revises unit 10's dance; the analysis needs units 5 and 7's comparing of styles and unit 7's criteria. Before unit 12, which stages the revised dance for two spaces | Candidate: every dance on the class list checked for a linked page that is open and free on the day the unit plan is written | A chosen dance is from the class list only, each read by the sensitivity agent with its page first. Línea Recta draws on flamenco's forms; the unit names Ballet Hispánico's own words for it. Critique of classmates names a criterion, never a body or looks. Hazards and controls: revisions keep every safety rule of unit 9 (no lifts, contact by agreement); floor checked. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
