he fancy shawl dance, and the band's earlier dances as review) show about their people's ideas and perspectives. Words: lead, staging, design, era, perspective, analyze. Showing: the two pieces staged in two spaces for families, with the family's yes for any showing outside class. End-of-unit evidence (two check days), id by id: DA:Pr6.1.8a, a guide look-for on the family showing (a warm-up led, etiquette kept, a note applied), with the kept rehearsal record; DA:Pr6.1.8b, the kept staging design for two spaces with the reasons, and a look-for on its use at the showing; DA:Cn10.1.8a, a written item on the written day: two dances linked and how they shape the student's or the invented dancer's view; DA:Cn10.1.8b, a guide look-for on the two pieces, with the kept research card's line on how the research shaped each; DA:Cn11.1.8a, a written item: what two dances of different cultures and eras show about their people's ideas, with one detail from each. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: a team research question answered in dance (Level 1). Tools: the lead card, staging card, research card, work cards, Curio Class's own. Free path: the room's own light and two spaces of the school. Once-a-week path: 6 sessions | Last in the band because the family showing gathers the year's shaping, group making and revision, and analyzing many eras needs the styles studied across the band. Its family showing (weeks 30 and 31) is its own end-of-unit assessment, before the year-end check in week 32; the threads then go to Level 1 | Candidate: the topic list checked for party politics and for any topic that asks a student to tell their own hardship | Powwow: grand entries open with ceremony and prayer; the fancy shawl dance is studied by watching, from the museum's and a tribal nation's own pages read first by the sensitivity agent; regalia is never called a costume and never worn or made by students. The Savoy: segregation is told in plain sentences from a museum's page; no r